   "subject": "Re: [r-inla] Spatial modeling of areal data",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "I do not see any questions here.\r<br>\r<br>Anyway, the books at <a href=\"http://www.r-inla.org\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=http://www.r-inla.org&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792434548469000&amp;usg=AOvVaw04SLguN0h7VvMMjHAotq8q\">www.r-inla.org</a> discuss these kind of models more\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On Sat, 2024-12-14 at 08:05 -0800, 廖芝瑜 wrote:\r<br>&gt; Hi INLA group,\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; I want  to estimate mortality rate or prevalence rate (crude rate) using\r<br>&gt; the R-INLA package to produce smoothed county level mortality rate or\r<br>&gt; prevalence rate (crude rate),  the model includes two random effects(spatially\r<br>&gt; structured and unstructured random effect), and I assume the response variable\r<br>&gt; Y(mortality rate) is lognormal distributed, how can I modify the code below:\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Neighborhood matrix\r<br>&gt; #We create the neighborhood matrix needed to define the spatial random effect\r<br>&gt; using the poly2nb() and the nb2INLA() functions \r<br>&gt; nb &lt;- poly2nb(MapData_368version)\r<br>&gt; nb2INLA(&quot;map.adj&quot;, nb)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; g &lt;- inla.read.graph(filename = &quot;map.adj&quot;) \r<br>&gt; #g which we will later use to specific the spatial random effect\r<br>&gt;  \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; Inference using INLA\r<br>&gt; #The model includes two random effects, for modeling the spatial residual\r<br>&gt; variation, and modeling unstructured noise. We need to include two vectors in\r<br>&gt; the data that denote the indices of these random effects. We call re_u the\r<br>&gt; indices vector and re_v the indices vector. We set both re_u and re_v  equal\r<br>&gt; to 1,...n is the number of counties. This can be obtained with the number of\r<br>&gt; rows in the data (nrow(map)).\r<br>&gt; map$re_u  &lt;- 1:nrow(map)\r<br>&gt; map$re_v  &lt;- 1:nrow(map)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; The response variable is Y. Random effects are defined using f() with\r<br>&gt; parameters equal to the name of the index variable and the chosen model.  We\r<br>&gt; use model = &quot;besag&quot; with neighborhood matrix given by g. We also use\r<br>&gt; option scale.model = TRUE to make the precision parameter of models with\r<br>&gt; different CAR priors comparable (Freni-Sterrantino, Ventrucci, and Rue 2018).\r<br>&gt; Then, we choose model = &quot;iid&quot;.\r<br>&gt; formula &lt;- CrudeR ~ f(re_u, model = &quot;besag&quot;, graph = g, scale.model = TRUE) +\r<br>&gt; f(re_v, model = &quot;iid&quot;)\r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; \r<br>&gt; We fit the model by calling the inla() function. We specify the formula,\r<br>&gt; family (&quot;lognormal&quot;), data. We also set control.predictor equal\r<br>&gt; to list(compute = TRUE) to compute the posteriors of the predictions.\r<br>&gt; res &lt;- inla(formula, family = &quot;lognormal&quot;, data = map,\r<br>&gt;               control.predictor = list(compute = TRUE),\r<br>&gt;               control.compute = list(dic = TRUE))\r<br>"
    ],
